According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Road Electronic Toll Collection System (ETC) market was valued at US$ 10,084.24 million in 2024 and is projected to reach US$ 15,026.52 million by 2031, growing at a CAGR of 5.85% during the forecast period (2025–2031). This growth is fueled by increasing urban congestion, government mandates for smart transportation infrastructure, and technological advancements in contactless payment systems.
What is a Road Electronic Toll Collection System (ETC)?
Road Electronic Toll Collection (ETC) refers to fully automated systems that enable cashless toll payments using technologies like RFID, GPS, and ANPR. These systems eliminate stop-and-go traffic at toll plazas while improving revenue collection efficiency by 40–60% compared to manual systems, according to transportation studies. Major implementations include RFID-based FASTag in India, DSRC-based E-ZPass in North America, and GNSS-based tolling in Singapore's ERP 2.0 system.
The market is witnessing a technological shift from traditional gantry-based systems to next-generation solutions like:
Distance-based GNSS tolling (India's 2024 implementation)
Dynamic pricing models (Dubai's Salik system launching in 2025)
Integrated mobility platforms combining tolling with parking and congestion charging
